\title{Temporal Prediction as a Pretext Task for Satellite Image Time Series}

% DOUBLE-BLIND: no \author block, no affiliation, no acknowledgements,
% no github.com/<username>. Anonymous code link only, or none at all.

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Self-supervised pretraining on satellite image time series is usually framed spatially: mask and
reconstruct, or contrast augmented views of a single acquisition. We ask whether predicting the
\emph{future latent} of a series is a better pretext task on PASTIS, and find that it is, under a
frozen-probe protocol matched across methods. We report the comparison against both a random-init and
a raw-feature floor, quantify the advantage as a function of label budget, and give evidence for the
mechanism: the temporal objective makes the \emph{spatial} representation season-aware. We are
explicit about what the experiment does and does not establish.
\end{abstract}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}
% ~0.5 page. Land three things and stop.
%  1. SITS is temporal; dominant SSL pretexts are not.
%  2. Predict-the-future-latent (JEPA-style, EMA target, stop-grad, narrow
%     predictor) is the natural temporal pretext, and needs anti-collapse
%     on SITS because consecutive acquisitions are near-identical.
%  3. Contributions, three bullets, no more.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}\itemsep0pt
  \item A temporal-JEPA pretext for SITS with a variance--covariance term that is \emph{necessary},
        not decorative: without it the objective collapses because the future frame is nearly the
        present one (Fig.~\ref{fig:erank}).
  \item A frozen-probe comparison on PASTIS against spatial JEPA, MAE, BYOL and SimCLR
        \emph{and against a random-init and a raw-feature floor} (Table~\ref{tab:main}).
  \item Evidence on where the advantage comes from: it grows as labels shrink
        (Fig.~\ref{fig:labeleff}), and the temporal objective makes the spatial features decode
        acquisition month far better than the spatial objective does.
\end{itemize}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}
% ~0.5 page. Factorized spatial ViT + temporal transformer; context =
% past acquisitions, target = latent of the frame Delta ahead under an EMA
% encoder with stop-grad; predictor narrower than the encoder (384 < 512).
% State the anti-collapse term explicitly with its coefficients.

Anti-collapse is the part that is specific to SITS. Standard JEPA (EMA target, stop-gradient, narrow
predictor) collapses here: consecutive acquisitions of a parcel are nearly identical, so
``predict the future latent'' is solved by emitting a constant. We add a VICReg-style
variance--covariance penalty on the trainable context embedding
($\lambda_v = 1.0$, $\lambda_c = 0.04$); setting both to zero recovers pure I-JEPA and reproduces the
collapse.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experimental setup}
% ~0.4 page. PASTIS, official folds. State the protocol in one paragraph
% and be precise -- V4 (comparison hygiene) is the thing reviewers check.

PASTIS \citep{garnot2021pastis}, official 5-fold split, 10 Sentinel-2 bands, $128\times128$ patches,
day-of-year positional encoding. All methods share the encoder architecture, the effective batch
(192), the optimizer, and the probe budget (15 epochs, both a linear and a convolutional head).
Encoders are frozen; only the head is trained. \textbf{All comparisons are on the same evaluation
split} --- \PENDING{state val or test and keep every row on it; the project previously probed one
architecture on test while every baseline was on val, producing a $-1.8$ mIoU artifact}.

\paragraph{Floors.} Two cells anchor the table. \texttt{random} is the identical architecture with
weights never updated, which isolates the objective from the inductive bias of temporal attention.
\texttt{raw\_features} is patch-mean raw bands with no encoder, the true no-representation floor.
\PENDING{P0 --- both cells}

