\paragraph{The advantage is the objective, not compute.} Spatial JEPA trained $3.5\times$ longer to
match wall-clock reaches \PENDING{P1; seed-0 value 15.6}, no better than standard spatial JEPA.

\paragraph{Mechanism, inline.} Probing the \emph{frozen spatial} features (not the temporal pathway,
which would be circular given DOY encoding) to decode acquisition time from a single frame: 12-way
month classification, chance $8.3\%$. \PENDING{regenerate via scripts/mechanistic.py --- previously
61.3\% vs 46.3\%, stdout-only, no committed artifact} Since crops separate by phenological stage,
this is direct evidence that the future-prediction objective made the \emph{spatial} representation
season-aware.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations}
% Keep this. One tight paragraph. Do NOT sell it as a contribution --
% it is here so the paper reads as visibly rigorous. Name, in order:
% the floors, n, the val-vs-test protocol, the SimCLR negative-count
% caveat, and the missing temporal-SSL baseline if P2 does not land.

Our comparison is frozen-probe only; we do not evaluate fine-tuning, and supervised U-TAE
($63.1$ mIoU) is a ceiling rather than a peer. Multi-seed results cover
\PENDING{list exactly which cells} at $n{=}5$; MAE, BYOL and SimCLR are single-seed and are marked as
such in Table~\ref{tab:main}. All cells are probed on \PENDING{val/test} with an identical budget.
Our SimCLR baseline uses the negative count afforded by the per-step batch, which is smaller than the
regime contrastive methods are usually tuned for, so it should be read as a same-budget baseline
rather than a tuned one. \PENDING{if P2 does not land: ``We do not compare against a non-predictive
temporal pretext, so we cannot separate the benefit of predicting future latents from the benefit of
using the temporal axis at all.''}

